-- 128_supergun_server_arch.surql -- evaluation of the CENTRAL AUTHORITATIVE SERVER proposal, plus two new
-- measurements (the real combat-frame write set; concurrent-tenancy scaling of the frame function).
-- Lane: senior-re-generalist / SUPERGUN SERVER ARCH. Even seed numbers belong to the SUPERGUN netcode family
-- (seeds 117, 119, 120, 122, 124); the ENGINE lane takes odd (121, 123, 127).
-- Source doc: mvc-live-skins-quarters/docs/SUPERGUN-SERVER-ARCH.md (new, this lane).
-- Method step 4 (tag and store). No new function matching was required; every address cited was already
-- CONFIRMED by seeds 106/117/119/121/123/127. Step 3 was used once: the per-frame write set is attributed to
-- blk field groups through the block map before any conclusion is drawn from it.
-- NOTHING IN THE SUPERGUN-NETCODE s9 DESIGN CHANGES. This seed records an evaluation and two measurements.
-- Apply with tools/re_kb/apply_seed.py after the README export backup
-- (re_kb_data/_exports/re_kb_20260904-162659_pre128.surql). Idempotent; explicit edge ids. No apostrophes.
-- Do NOT re-run 07_dedup_edges.surql afterwards.
USE NS re DB kb;

UPSERT source:supergun_server_arch_20260904 SET kind='analysis', strength='reproduction', ref='mvc-live-skins-quarters/docs/SUPERGUN-SERVER-ARCH.md; tools d3dcap/net/blk_delta.py and d3dcap/net/sg_tenancy.py; inputs are the existing per-tick blk dumps of the Gate 2 stage-9 run (TEMP/rrcap_runner/gate2_300, 301 dumps) and the ENGINE lane Gate 4 Carnival run (TEMP/rr_g4/run_cd00, 300 dumps)', note='Evaluation of a proposed architecture, carrying two new measurements of its own. Machine: AMD Ryzen 9 5900XT, 16 cores, 512 KB L2 per core, 64 MB L3, DDR4-3200 dual channel (51.2 GB/s peak), Windows 11 26200. BYOR: all game-derived inputs and outputs under TEMP, nothing committed.';

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- NEW MEASUREMENT 1: the combat-frame write set (this was the load-bearing unknown behind state streaming)
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
UPSERT finding:supergun_combat_frame_write_set SET text='CONFIRMED and NEW 2026-09-04. FRAME-READSET s0(iii) measured 120 changed blk bytes on ONE IDLE tick by p-code emulation; the COMBAT-frame write set had never been measured. Measured now by differencing consecutive per-tick blk dumps (rr_runner --dump-every 1) with d3dcap/net/blk_delta.py, on two stages, 600 frames total. STAGE 9, 300 real receipt ticks, verified to be genuine combat (P2 health 144 to 106; drawn-node count 50 to 79 at ticks 145 to 175): blk bytes CHANGED per frame min 88, p50 363, p90 736, p99 1553, max 2515; whole-region XOR then zlib-6 (the simplest possible encoder) min 437, p50 814, p90 1336, p99 1746, max 2844. CARNIVAL (stage 3), 300 ticks: changed min 466, p50 665, p90 904, p99 1252, max 3160; XOR+zlib p50 1315, max 2325. AT 60 Hz THAT IS 49 TO 171 KB PER SECOND on stage 9 and 79 TO 140 KB PER SECOND on Carnival. Attribution through the block map: object-pool nodes (blk+0x6DD8, 256 x 0x280) 66.4 percent on stage 9 and 80.2 percent on Carnival; fighters (blk+0x3DB8, 6 x 0x738) 13.4 / 8.1; stage-camera (blk+0x6908) 7.9 / 1.7; render-list records (blk+0x1000) 5.1 / 5.8; battle state (blk+0x32500) 4.0 / 2.3. CONCLUSION: the per-frame authoritative state delta is ORDER 1 KB -- not order-100 bytes and not tens of kilobytes. Stage dependence is confirmed again (Carnival p50 is 1.8x stage 9), consistent with the stage dependence of the SAVE-SET requirement found by Gate 4.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_combat_write_set_no_super SET text='SCOPE LIMIT on finding:supergun_combat_frame_write_set, recorded so the number is not over-quoted. NEITHER measured run contains a hyper combo, a DHC or a full-screen super; the largest event measured is a 79-drawn-node effect burst. The bound that makes the conclusion robust anyway is INFERRED, not measured: the pool holds 256 nodes ((0x2EDF0-0x6DD8)/0x280), and the node share of the worst measured frame is about 66 percent of 2515 B, about 1660 B across about 79 drawn nodes, so a fully saturated pool scales that to about 5.4 KB and a worst-frame total of roughly 6 to 8 KB, about 370 to 480 KB per second at 60 Hz -- large but not disqualifying. FALSIFICATION: run blk_delta.py over a 300-tick receipt whose inputs contain a hyper combo and a DHC; if the p99 per-frame delta exceeds about 8 KB the order-1-KB claim is WRONG and state streaming must be re-costed. Tracked as U-S2.';

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- NEW MEASUREMENT 2: concurrent tenancy of the real frame function
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
UPSERT finding:supergun_server_tenancy_measured SET text='CONFIRMED and NEW 2026-09-04, d3dcap/net/sg_tenancy.py: K REAL rr_runner processes on the same anchor, 9000 ticks each with real inputs, per-tick timings pooled. This is the actual frame function under actual contention, not a synthetic proxy. Per-tick ms (p50 / p99 / max): K=1 0.0430 / 0.1256 / 0.415 ; K=4 0.0513 / 0.1494 / 4.13 ; K=8 0.0562 / 0.1594 / 6.24 ; K=16 0.0590 / 0.2235 / 26.5 ; K=24 0.0591 / 0.2323 / 59.1 ; K=32 0.0591 / 0.2206 / 96.0. So p50 degrades only 37 percent from 1 to 32 concurrent simulations and then FLATTENS, while p99 degrades 76 percent. THE MAX COLUMN IS THE IMPORTANT ONE AND IT IS NOT AN ENGINE PROPERTY: 96 ms is OS scheduler preemption of 32 unpinned unprioritised processes on a 32-thread desktop. Density arithmetic that follows: sim only at K=32 gives about 282 matches per core at 60 Hz, but adding zero-delay rollback at depth 8 (Gate N1b per-event p50 0.375 ms, p99 0.863 ms) collapses it to 38 at p50 and 18 at p99 -- THE ROLLBACK TERM, NOT THE TICK, SETS SERVER DENSITY, at 10 to 60 times the tick. Caveat recorded: the looped input file drives the sim off-script after 300 ticks and faults at about tick 9960 (0xc0000005 reading 0x38 at RVA 0x2704c8); that is the input file, NOT an engine defect, runs were capped at 9000 ticks, and the fault must not be cited as anything.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_server_memory_and_singleton SET text='CONFIRMED 2026-09-04. (a) One rr_runner instance peaks at 108 MB working set and 597 MB commit (polled WorkingSet64 / PrivateMemorySize64); the commit is the runner MEM_COMMIT of a 256 MiB arena plus its bump heap, which a server would reserve rather than commit. (b) OVER 300 COMBAT TICKS THE TICK DIRTIES ONLY 16 UNIQUE 4 KiB DC-RAM PAGES -- 64 KB of a 32 MB image (from the --harvest-dump dcram_tNNN.dlt records): DC 0x0CE60000-0x0CE67000 (texture decompress buffer), 0x0D00B000, 0x0D01B000, 0x0D082000, 0x0D086000, 0x0D089000, 0x0D853000, 0x0D855000 (the two animated props Gate 4 classified PARTIAL); per tick p50 3 pages, max 9. So DC-RAM is 99.8 percent read-only during a match. INFERRED from that: copy-on-write sharing of one 32 MB image across tenants with the same roster order and stage would collapse the private per-match footprint to roughly blk 212 KB + ctx 4 MB + about 64 KB of COW DC-RAM + staging, about 5 MB, against a shared image set of about 100 MB. NOT demonstrated on a COW-shared host -- tracked as U-S5. (c) STRUCTURAL, CONFIRMED: the recompiled image is a SINGLETON. rr_runner.cpp:709 refuses any base but 0x140000000 (the image is position-dependent, contract C1) and the arena is a 256 MiB region at a fixed VA with a fixed layout. There is NO instance handle, so a headless multi-match server cannot hold two live matches in one address space without swapping state in and out; tenancy is one PROCESS per match, or a state swap of at least 277 KB per match switch at about 4.2 microseconds each way. Not an obstacle, but a design constraint nobody had written down.';

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE VERDICTS
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
UPSERT finding:supergun_server_authority_loses_on_latency SET text='VERDICT on the central-authoritative-server proposal, and it needs no measurement to hold. SUPERGUN-NETCODE s9 architecture E races DIRECT and RELAY on every packet and takes the first arrival, so its latency is min(direct, relay) BY CONSTRUCTION. A server-authoritative design must route every input through the authority, so its latency is relay, ALWAYS. min(direct, relay) is less than or equal to relay is not an empirical claim, therefore SERVER AUTHORITY CANNOT BEAT THE ALREADY-SPECIFIED PEER DESIGN ON LATENCY; it can only tie, and only when the relay is on the path. Gate N4 and Gate N3 decide a DIFFERENT question -- whether our path beats the path Steam actually uses -- and that question applies equally to both architectures, so no outcome of either gate can break this tie. Supporting geometry (great-circle distance at fibre propagation, about 200000 km/s, hard floors): LA-NY direct floor 39.4 ms; relayed via Chicago 39.5 ms (+0.3 percent), Denver +0.5, Ashburn +1.7, Dallas +6.6, Atlanta +9.5, Portland +33.6. THE DISQUALIFYING CASE IS NOT CROSS-COUNTRY BUT SAME-REGION PLAY THROUGH AN OFF-PATH AUTHORITY: an LA-LA pair relayed via a single central NY server pays a 78.7 ms propagation FLOOR against about 0 ms direct, and LA-Seattle pays 78.0 ms against 15.5. A single central authoritative server is therefore disqualifying for a continental player base; a fleet with per-match server selection fixes the geometry, at which point it is the same relay fleet architecture E already needs for NAT, but with the min() removed.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_state_streaming_verdict SET text='VERDICT on streaming authoritative STATE instead of INPUTS. Bandwidth, measured both sides: shipped GGPO inputs about 4.1 KB per second (32 to 40 B payload plus 28 B IP/UDP at 60 Hz, from PacketSize FUN_14011df90); the s9 redundant 64-frame window at 120 Hz about 34.6 KB per second; the authoritative blk delta 49 to 171 KB per second (finding:supergun_combat_frame_write_set); the RENDER-SUFFICIENT stream, which already exists as the TAPE, 33 to 72 KB per second (runner_tape.json.gz is 164191 B for 301 frames including one-time anchors, about 545 B per frame amortised, per-frame section about 1205 B uncompressed, consistent with the live-tape rate of 1.8 to 3.1 MB per minute). SO STATE STREAMING DOES NOT DIE ON BANDWIDTH -- it costs 12 to 40 times the shipped input stream and 0.4 to 1.4 Mbit per second per client, cheaper than a 720p video stream by an order of magnitude. IT DIES ON LATENCY: a client that does not simulate locally sees its OWN input only after a full round trip, so own-input added latency is about 10 to 27 ms for a player in the server metro, 40 to 57 ms one region away and 70 to 87 ms cross-country to a central authority, each plus a 0 to 16.7 ms tick-boundary quantisation -- i.e. THE SAME BAND AS THE 66.7 ms STEAM ALREADY SHIPS, and jittery where Steam is constant. Against the s9 zero-delay design that is a 40 to 90 ms REGRESSION. Where state streaming IS right: spectating and THE RAIL (a spectator has no input, so the round-trip objection vanishes), money-match receipts and a witness, and resync beyond the rollback ring -- with the ENGINE lane constraint that a state transfer does not carry the static geometry the renderer reads, so the receiver must already hold it from its own arc.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_xdp_ebpf_verdict SET text='VERDICT on the XDP / eBPF / DPDK part of the proposal. (1) RUNNING THE FRAME FUNCTION IN eBPF IS IMPOSSIBLE, three independent ways, each sufficient. THE BPF INSTRUCTION SET HAS NO FLOATING POINT: it defines eight classes -- LD, LDX, ST, STX, ALU (32-bit integer), JMP, JMP32, ALU64 -- and no floating-point class or opcode exists (kernel.org BPF instruction-set standard), while the MvC2 frame is float-heavy throughout and rr_runner carries an --fma switch precisely because FMA contraction changes results. COMPLEXITY: the verifier BPF_COMPLEXITY_LIMIT_INSNS is 1000000 verified instructions and one MvC2 tick executes 459681 instructions (FRAME-READSET s0), the same ORDER as the entire verifier budget, for a program that must also be proven to terminate on every path while its control flow is data-dependent. STACK: all BPF program types are limited to 512 bytes of stack, against 211736 bytes of flat, pointer-addressed state. Recompiling the frame to BPF is not an escape either: an integer-only ISA would change float results and break the byte gate the whole project rests on. (2) THE PRACTICAL VERSION IS REAL BUT SERVER-SIDE ONLY: AF_XDP, DPDK, pinned isolated cores and hugepages are Linux mechanisms, and the client is the Windows Steam binary (position-dependent x86-64 PE pinned at 0x140000000), so the client packet path stays a Windows socket whatever we do. (3) IT IS WORTH ABOUT 0.01 TO 0.03 ms PER PACKET (INFERRED, bounded above by the measured whole OS socket path of 0.057 ms loopback RTT and 0.023 ms per sendto), against 66.7 ms of shipped frame delay -- a factor of about 2200, the smallest term on the list. BUILD IT LAST IF AT ALL.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_cache_residency_proven SET text='CONFIRMED BY ARITHMETIC, and it vindicates the cache half of the proposal. A full 211736 B save takes 0.0042 ms (SUPERGUN-NETCODE s1.2), which is 50.4 GB per second of COPY, i.e. about 101 GB per second of read-plus-write memory traffic. This machine has DDR4-3200 in dual channel, a theoretical peak of 51.2 GB per second. 101 is greater than 51.2, THEREFORE THE COPY CANNOT BE SERVED FROM DRAM AND IS CACHE-RESIDENT. Note the framing correction: L2 on this CPU is 512 KB PER CORE (8 MB across 16 cores), not the 1 to 2 MB assumed, so the right statement is not that the 212 KB state block fits in L2 but that the TICK WORKING SET IS SMALL -- from FRAME-READSET s3 the tick reads about 36 KB (blk 10004, DC-RAM 22545, exe 2445, ctx 1871) and writes about 127 KB (6692 + 11488 + 716 + 43217 + about 64 KB staging), about 160 KB touched per frame against 512 KB of L2. The instinct is right; the mechanism is the working set, not the state size.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_pin_and_isolate_required SET text='THE LARGEST MEASURED WIN IN THE SERVER-ARCH EVALUATION, and it is not networking. finding:supergun_server_tenancy_measured recorded a 96 ms per-tick MAX at K=32 unpinned tick processes, against a p50 of 0.0591 ms -- OS scheduler preemption injecting a stall LARGER THAN THE 66.7 ms FRAME DELAY THE WHOLE PROJECT EXISTS TO DELETE, and three orders of magnitude larger than anything kernel bypass could recover (about 0.03 ms). REQUIREMENT: any host that ticks a match -- relay, witness, or a future authoritative server -- must PIN ITS TICK THREADS TO ISOLATED CORES. This is the actionable form of the pinned-core instinct in the proposal, it costs a configuration change, and it applies to the s9 peer design just as much as to any server design.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_ggpo_validation_is_a_misnomer SET text='NAMING VERDICT. GGPO is three things: an input-prediction and ROLLBACK engine, a frame-advantage clock (TimeSync), and a UDP protocol. Its VALUE is the first. VALIDATION IS NOT A GGPO CONCEPT AT ALL -- upstream has no validation path and the Steam build is upstream verbatim with only the transport replaced (CONFIRMED, seed 117). Under server authority, prediction and rollback either move to the server (in which case the design is server-authoritative rollback with client-side prediction, and GGPO transport -- the part s9 replaces -- does not survive) or they disappear (in which case nothing is left to call GGPO, and input validation means a sequence number and a signature, a transport concern). ACCURATE NAMES: what was proposed is AUTHORITATIVE LOCKSTEP WITH A SIGNED INPUT CHANNEL; the version that works is SERVER-AUTHORITATIVE ROLLBACK WITH CLIENT-SIDE PREDICTION; what s9 specifies is PEER ROLLBACK WITH ZERO INPUT DELAY AND DEEP SPECULATION. Do not keep the GGPO label on the authoritative design: it would smuggle in the assumption that prediction survives, which the proposal as stated does not contain.';

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE FOUR VARIANTS
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
UPSERT finding:supergun_v1_broker_transport_verdict SET text='V1 VERDICT -- Redis or NATS pub/sub as the INPUT transport: NO for inputs, YES (already) for everything else, and the boundary is the point. AGAINST INPUTS on a 16.667 ms deadline: TCP head-of-line blocking means one lost segment stalls every message behind it for at least one RTT, whereas in a rollback scheme a lost input packet costs NOTHING because the next packet carries the redundant window -- so a TCP broker INVERTS the single most important property of the transport; a broker is by definition store-and-forward, i.e. an extra hop with a userspace parse-match-publish cycle; and without TCP_NODELAY the sender coalesces small writes, and every input packet is small. THE IRONY THAT MUST BE STATED: seed 119 CONFIRMED that the shipped game already pays a send-buffering delay because it passes k_EP2PSendUnreliable at all five SendP2PPacket sites, so INTERPOSING A TCP BROKER ADDS MORE BUFFERING ON TOP OF THE ONE STRUCTURAL DEFECT WE HAVE ACTUALLY PROVEN -- the opposite direction from the thing SUPERGUN exists to delete. WHERE THEY ARE RIGHT, which is most of the system: lobby and matchmaking, invitations, spectator fan-out, THE RAIL odds and bet flow, result and receipt delivery, leaderboard and ladder updates, agent telemetry -- all human-timescale, all fan-out shaped, and we already run Redis pub/sub for the real-time feed bus. THE BOUNDARY: the 60 Hz input and state path is bespoke UDP and nothing else touches it; every event whose deadline is a human patience rather than a frame goes through the broker we already run.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_v2_is_rollback_happy_path SET text='V2 VERDICT -- if the prediction is verified we keep playing: THIS IS ROLLBACK ITS HAPPY PATH, NOT AN ALTERNATIVE TO IT, and the design already has it. GGPO predicts the remote input (repeat-last), runs the frame, and when the real input arrives it compares: if it MATCHES, nothing is rewound and the cost is EXACTLY ZERO. Only a misprediction triggers restore-and-resim. CONFIRMED against the vendored upstream (sync.cpp, input_queue.cpp) which the Steam build carries verbatim. The mechanism was described correctly; it is already specified in SUPERGUN-NETCODE s9.8 as prediction = repeat-last in v1. The measured price of the OTHER branch is the Gate N1b depth-8 per-event 0.375 ms p50 and 0.863 ms p99, i.e. 2 to 5 percent of a frame. The open question this points at is not WHETHER to do it but HOW OFTEN THE PREDICTION IS WRONG AND HOW VISIBLE IT IS -- Gate N2, still open, and now the highest-value open gate in the netcode lane because it decides how far zero input delay can be pushed.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_v3_snap_verdict SET text='V3 VERDICT -- server-authoritative with NO rollback, injecting state and snapping characters into place: NO for any playing client. (a) INPUT DELAY COLLAPSES TO NETWORK LATENCY: about 10 to 27 ms in the server metro, 40 to 57 ms one region away, 70 to 87 ms cross-country to a central authority, each plus a 0 to 16.7 ms tick-boundary quantisation -- comparable to the 66.7 ms Steam already ships and JITTERY WHERE STEAM IS CONSTANT, i.e. a 40 to 90 ms regression against the s9 zero-delay design; it gives back the entire prize. (b) SNAPPING IS NOT THE TOLERABLE NUDGE IT IS IN A SHOOTER, for mechanical rather than aesthetic reasons: hit detection is DISCRETE BOX OVERLAP EVALUATED ON A SPECIFIC FRAME, so a two-frame positional correction does not look slightly off, it changes WHETHER THE MOVE CONNECTED and whether a 40 percent damage chain happened; the engine has no tolerance concept anywhere, which is exactly why every gate in this project is byte-exact. (c) SMOOTHING IS NOT AVAILABLE WITHOUT LOCAL SIMULATION: every visible quantity a correction would have to blend -- animation cell node+0x191, sprite id +0x188, placement +0x124..0x137, palette bank +0x39, hitstop and flash +0x170..0x175 -- is PRODUCED BY THE SIM (FRAME-READSET s3.1, TAPE-V3-SPEC s10). Our renderer can draw an arbitrary placement, so a position could be lerped, but cell, sprite id and hit flags would be stale and the character would SLIDE WITHOUT ANIMATING, making the correction more visible rather than less. Therefore: to smooth you must simulate locally; to simulate locally is to predict; to predict is to diverge; to diverge is to need reconciliation -- and that is rollback. V3 is rollback with the reconciliation step deleted and the latency paid in full. (d) NOTE THAT V3 PASSES THE BANDWIDTH GATE: the measured delta is 49 to 171 KB per second, so it does NOT die on bandwidth; it dies on (a) and (b), which is a stronger and more durable reason.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_v4_adaptive_delay_verdict SET text='V4 VERDICT -- dynamically adjust the delay to keep both sides in sync: this is ADAPTIVE DELAY-BASED NETCODE, GGPO already does a version of it, and on our own measured evidence it does it BADLY. Shipped, CONFIRMED: TimeSync::recommend_frame_wait_duration (FUN_14011cdc0, constants 40/10/3/9) drives FUN_140119010 case 5, which calls KERNEL32!Sleep(frames_ahead * 1000/60) -- a BLOCKING sleep of up to 150 ms on the game thread -- and seed 119 established that the input to that decision is a FRAME-QUANTISED RTT at about 16.67 ms granularity against a MIN_FRAME_ADVANTAGE threshold of 3, so the shipped adaptive-delay mechanism is driven by a clock that cannot resolve its own decision threshold. THE HUMAN ARGUMENT, WITH EVIDENCE AND ITS LIMIT: constant delays up to about 300 ms were tolerated while adding jitter to a 200 ms delay made lag noticed more often, hindered improvement with practice and increased failure rates (ACM SAP, Player perception of delays and jitter in character responsiveness); but SMALL local latency variation does NOT measurably affect performance (Schmid and Halbhuber 2023); and mean latency and jitter amplitude and frequency each independently degrade target acquisition (ACM TAP 2024). ALL OF THAT IS FPS AND AIMING LITERATURE, NOT FIGHTING GAMES -- the transfer is INFERRED and is tracked as U-S7. THE SYNTHESIS, ALREADY ENCODED IN s9.5: the evidence does not say never adapt, it says adapt in steps small enough to sit under the perceptual floor. Adapting the CLOCK continuously at 1 percent of the frame period (0.17 ms per frame, expressible at the measured 10 microsecond spin-to-deadline precision): yes, already specified. Adapting the INPUT DELAY in whole 16.7 ms frames: no, that is exactly the jitter the literature penalises. Frame delay stays a per-player user-set CONSTANT.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_two_mode_verdict SET text='VERDICT on the two-mode synthesis (peer rollback for casual and ranked, server-authoritative for money matches): COHERENT AND CORRECTLY MOTIVATED, BUT RECOMMEND NOT BUILDING IT NOW. What is genuinely right: server authority is the only mechanism on the table that makes cheating STRUCTURALLY IMPOSSIBLE rather than DETECTABLE -- under peer rollback both peers hold authoritative state and every peer-side detector is heuristic, so desync detection catches an INCONSISTENT lie and never a CONSISTENT one, whereas under authority the client can only lie about its own pad word, which is the one thing it is entitled to decide. WHY NOT NOW, and this is a process argument: (1) the cost is in the GATES, not the code -- N1b, N1c, N2 and N5 would each have to be run and held for BOTH paths, and this project stated failure mode is conclusions verified against the wrong thing; (2) the deciding gate for the DEFAULT design has not run (N2 decides how far zero delay can be pushed), and opening a second architecture first is the wrong order; (3) THE THREAT MODEL IS NOT STATED ANYWHERE -- no document quantifies the money-match cheat rate, and building a netcode path against an unquantified threat is the same error as optimising an unmeasured latency term (tracked as U-S6); (4) A MUCH CHEAPER MECHANISM GETS MOST OF THE BENEFIT: run the money match on the PEER design and additionally stream its state to a WITNESS server at 49 to 171 KB per second (or 33 to 72 using the tape), where the receipt runner -- which Gate 2 proved reproduces the live tape over 301 frames with zero unexplained differences -- re-simulates from the same inputs and byte-compares. That gives byte-exact divergence detection with runner_gate.py unchanged, a signed replayable receipt the platform already sells, ZERO added player latency because the witness is off the critical path, and reuse of gated machinery with no second netcode path. WHAT NEITHER APPROACH SOLVES: an impossible-input cheat whose pad words both peers accept -- the authority would also have to judge whether a pad sequence is human, so that is an input-plausibility problem orthogonal to where authority lives, and the two-mode design must not be credited with solving it.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_server_arch_recommendation SET text='RECOMMENDATION of the SUPERGUN SERVER ARCH evaluation. (1) KEEP SUPERGUN-NETCODE s9 AS THE DESIGN -- nothing in the evaluation changes it, and the structural argument (min(direct, relay) is at most relay) means a peer design that races the two paths cannot be beaten on latency by one that forces the relay. (2) DO NOT BUILD A SERVER-AUTHORITATIVE NETCODE PATH; do not build a second netcode path at all yet. (3) ADOPT ONE THING FROM THE PROPOSAL IMMEDIATELY AND IT IS NOT NETWORKING -- PIN AND ISOLATE (see finding:supergun_pin_and_isolate_required): a measured 96 ms scheduler stall is larger than the 66.7 ms this project exists to delete, and it is the largest measured win in the document. (4) BUILD THE WITNESS, NOT THE AUTHORITY, if and when trust work is prioritised. (5) KERNEL BYPASS LAST, server-side only, after N3 and N4, worth about 0.03 ms. (6) GATE ORDER UNCHANGED: N2 next because it decides the default design, then N4 because it is the only thing that converts our-transport-beats-Steam from a hope into a number; N1b and N1c are now CLOSED by the ENGINE lane (arm C passes on both stages, 0 events over the 16.667 ms budget at any depth, worst single frame 9.51 ms at depth 120). AND THE FRAMING IS UNCHANGED SINCE s0 OF THE NETCODE DOC: the 66.7 ms frame delay is still the dominant term and is still an ENGINE-enabled policy win, not a networking one.';

